{
  "reconstructed_theorem_text": "For every pair of real numbers a and b, with either sign and with zero allowed, the following inequality holds: (a^2 log(a^2) + b^2 log(b^2))/2 - ((a^2 + b^2)/2) log((a^2 + b^2)/2) <= (a - b)^2/2. Squares are natural-number powers in the real numbers. Here log is the real logarithm with log(0) = 0, so a zero square contributes zero to t log(t). There are no premises beyond a,b being real numbers. The coefficient 1/2 is fixed universally. The formula is defined also when a = 0, b = 0, or both are zero; when both are zero both sides vanish. Negative inputs remain admitted, and the right-hand side uses their actual signed difference. In particular a = b gives equality with both sides zero.",
  "objects": "Two scalars a and b; their real natural squares; their average square (a^2+b^2)/2; the real logarithm extended by log(0)=0; and the literal real signed-difference square (a-b)^2.",
  "domains": "a and b range over all of R. All arithmetic, division by 2, logarithms and the order comparison are real-valued. The arguments of log are nonnegative real squares or their nonnegative average; zero is included and evaluated with log(0)=0.",
  "quantifiers": "Universally for every a in R and independently every b in R, the displayed inequality holds. There are no existentially chosen constants, auxiliary objects or certificate parameters.",
  "assumptions": "No assumptions other than the declared real-number types of a and b. Neither positivity nor nonzero values, regularity, probability structure, nor any supplied inequality is required. The logarithm convention log(0)=0 is approved definition context, not a hypothesis on a or b.",
  "conclusion": "The average of the two terms a^2 log(a^2) and b^2 log(b^2), minus the average square multiplied by the logarithm of that same average square, is at most one half of (a-b)^2. This is a single non-strict real inequality.",
  "scopes": "Every occurrence of a and b in the conclusion refers to the same universally bound pair. The average (a^2+b^2)/2 occurs identically as prefactor and logarithm argument. All factors 1/2 are explicit; no normalization, measure, domain restriction or extra premise is implicit. Zero arguments of log and signed inputs are within this universal scope.",
  "constant_dependencies": "The only numeric normalization is the literal fixed denominator 2, equivalently coefficient 1/2. It depends on neither a nor b; no variable constant or hidden parameter appears.",
